1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

2
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:09,715
I remember that morning exactly.
I was drawing the Sun.

3
00:00:10,185 --> 00:00:12,520
Who draws at six in the morning?

4
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:14,150
MIRA: Artists.

5
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:17,375
And Biscuit was planning a crime.

6
00:00:17,845 --> 00:00:21,030
At that moment, half the
world was in sunlight.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,160
A farmer was checking the sky for rain.

8
00:00:24,630 --> 00:00:27,415
A boy was one second late for his bus.

9
00:00:27,885 --> 00:00:29,275
Biscuit, no!

10
00:00:29,885 --> 00:00:31,620
Then the Sun went out.

11
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:37,430
MIRA: Is it night again?

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:40,085
It's seven in the morning.

13
00:00:40,955 --> 00:00:43,565
Then someone knocked. Three times.

14
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:47,950
MR. ITO: Good morning. Shoes
on, please. Both of you.

15
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:50,780
Mr. Ito, from upstairs.

16
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:55,665
He had a torch, a radio, and a bag
he'd packed the night before.

17
00:00:56,135 --> 00:00:59,050
MR. ITO: And bring the dog. And the toast.

18
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:02,010
It took me a year to understand why.

19
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:06,040
Outside, the whole street was looking up.

20
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:07,820
It's an eclipse!

21
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:12,800
MR. ITO: Eclipses are polite. They
leave after seven minutes.

22
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:15,685
Roof. Bring the telescope.

23
00:01:16,155 --> 00:01:20,340
Kai carried it up six floors. He
complained about every one.

24
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:22,575
MR. ITO: Venus first.

25
00:01:23,045 --> 00:01:24,860
MIRA: Why is that one still on?

26
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:28,520
MR. ITO: Venus shines with
the Sun's light.

27
00:01:28,990 --> 00:01:32,805
That light takes the long
way round to reach us.

28
00:01:33,275 --> 00:01:38,765
If something happened at the Sun, Venus
goes dark in three and a half minutes.

29
00:01:39,235 --> 00:01:40,625
So we waited.

30
00:01:44,495 --> 00:01:47,085
MR. ITO: Then it happened at the Sun.

31
00:01:47,955 --> 00:01:52,120
And Jupiter will go out at
four minutes past eight.

32
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:55,055
At four minutes past eight, it did.

33
00:01:55,925 --> 00:01:59,565
He always tapped it three
times. I never asked why.

34
00:02:09,435 --> 00:02:11,950
By nine, it was a degree colder.

35
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:16,760
(RADIO) Phone networks are down
across the city. Stay indoors.

36
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:19,390
Everyone's going to the shops.

37
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:22,100
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

38
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:26,035
Kai was right. The whole
city had the same idea.

39
00:02:26,505 --> 00:02:29,020
Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

40
00:02:29,490 --> 00:02:30,930
MIRA: They're on my list.

41
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:34,330
Two grown men fought over the last heater.

42
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:38,265
MR. ITO: Heaters need
electricity. They'll learn.

43
00:02:38,735 --> 00:02:43,675
Kai, Mira, it's Mum. They need me at
the hospital. Stay with Mr. Ito.

44
00:02:44,145 --> 00:02:47,230
Mum was a nurse. She'd been
on the night shift.

45
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:49,465
Is the Sun… dead?

46
00:02:50,235 --> 00:02:53,775
MR. ITO: The Moon pulls the
sea. So does the Sun.

47
00:02:54,245 --> 00:02:58,760
If the Sun were gone, today's tide
would be a third smaller.

48
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:00,895
Go and look.

49
00:03:01,365 --> 00:03:04,055
So, Kai and I walked down to the harbour.

50
00:03:04,525 --> 00:03:06,485
MIRA: It's exactly on the line!

51
00:03:06,955 --> 00:03:08,915
So the Sun's still there.

52
00:03:09,385 --> 00:03:10,795
MIRA: Then where is it?

53
00:03:11,565 --> 00:03:15,425
The Sun hadn't gone anywhere.
Something was in front of it.

54
00:03:16,395 --> 00:03:19,560
MIRA: The sea's growing a
skin. The fish don't mind!

55
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:23,415
On Day 3, the sky did something beautiful.

56
00:03:23,885 --> 00:03:29,200
(RADIO) …a solar storm that left the Sun
four days ago, still arriving tonight.

57
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:31,335
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

58
00:03:39,005 --> 00:03:41,715
On Day 7, the power went out.

59
00:03:42,185 --> 00:03:45,500
MR. ITO: Fill the bath.
Before the pipes freeze.

60
00:03:45,970 --> 00:03:49,380
Mr. Ito showed us how to
make one warm room.

61
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:52,435
Free fridge. Finally, some good news.

62
00:03:52,905 --> 00:03:55,890
That night, Kai pretended
he wasn't scared.

63
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:58,775
He was holding my hand too hard.

64
00:03:59,745 --> 00:04:01,485
Then we smelled smoke.

65
00:04:01,955 --> 00:04:05,170
MR. ITO: They're burning
furniture. Indoors.

66
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:10,080
Everyone out. The smoke you can
see. The gas you can't.

67
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:13,135
Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

68
00:04:13,605 --> 00:04:16,165
At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

69
00:04:16,635 --> 00:04:17,770
MIRA: BISCUIT!

70
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:20,905
Biscuit! Come on, boy!

71
00:04:21,375 --> 00:04:25,515
Kai searched all night. Then
again, it was always night now.

72
00:04:28,185 --> 00:04:30,270
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

73
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:33,725
They led to the only warm
thing on the street.

74
00:04:34,195 --> 00:04:36,360
You stupid, brilliant dog.

75
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:40,890
A few metres down, the ground
stays around ten degrees.

76
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:44,920
Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd
found the way down.

77
00:04:45,390 --> 00:04:48,975
By morning, half the street had
followed him underground.

78
00:04:49,445 --> 00:04:53,230
By Day 30, Harbour Street
station was a town.

79
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:58,435
We grew food under pink lamps.
Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

80
00:04:58,905 --> 00:05:03,290
At seven every morning, the
engineers turned on the big lamps.

81
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:05,900
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

82
00:05:06,370 --> 00:05:10,030
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And
it's warm on your face.

83
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:13,785
Some of them had already
forgotten what colour it was.

84
00:05:14,855 --> 00:05:18,020
Biscuit got a fan club.
And a bigger belly.

85
00:05:27,090 --> 00:05:31,455
Mr. Ito spent weeks rebuilding
a firefighter's camera.

86
00:05:31,925 --> 00:05:37,365
MR. ITO: It sees heat, not light. If
something's out there, it has to be warm.

87
00:05:37,835 --> 00:05:41,070
On Day 40, he took us up to the surface.

88
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:44,175
He pointed it where the Sun
should have been.

89
00:05:44,645 --> 00:05:46,160
MR. ITO: It's warm.

90
00:05:47,130 --> 00:05:50,520
And it's twice as wide as the Sun.

91
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:53,125
There was something around the Sun.

92
00:05:53,595 --> 00:05:55,910
A shell. Wrapped around a star.

93
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:58,465
MIRA: Who built it?

94
00:05:59,335 --> 00:06:01,845
Nobody could build that in a day.

95
00:06:02,315 --> 00:06:04,430
MR. ITO: No. Nobody did.

96
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:08,565
Nineteen-nineteen. Look at the lines.

97
00:06:09,035 --> 00:06:13,600
It was always there. Edge-on,
like the slats of a blind.

98
00:06:14,070 --> 00:06:18,210
And on the morning the Sun went
out, someone closed it.

99
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:21,170
MIRA: You've seen those lines before?

100
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:26,355
MR. ITO: I saw them once. A long
time ago. Nobody listened.

101
00:06:27,225 --> 00:06:29,865
I believed him. It was even true.

102
00:06:30,935 --> 00:06:34,300
On Day 100, the generators failed.

103
00:06:34,770 --> 00:06:38,135
MR. ITO: I know a little
about old machines.

104
00:06:38,605 --> 00:06:40,565
That was the coldest night.

105
00:06:41,035 --> 00:06:43,100
How does this end, Mira?

106
00:06:44,370 --> 00:06:46,385
MIRA: With the lights coming back on.

107
00:06:47,455 --> 00:06:49,970
On Day 180, they did.

108
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:52,055
And so did Mum.

109
00:06:53,325 --> 00:06:55,215
You kept them alive.

110
00:06:55,685 --> 00:06:57,675
MR. ITO: They kept me alive.

111
00:06:58,545 --> 00:07:00,005
MIRA: What are you building?

112
00:07:00,545 --> 00:07:02,760
MR. ITO: Something to listen with.

113
00:07:03,630 --> 00:07:07,815
By Day 300, his camera had
a much bigger lens.

114
00:07:08,285 --> 00:07:11,145
A thin, warm thread was leaving the shell.

115
00:07:11,615 --> 00:07:16,080
MR. ITO: It's sending the light away.
Out of the plane of the planets.

116
00:07:16,550 --> 00:07:18,065
Sending it where?

117
00:07:21,150 --> 00:07:22,790
MR. ITO: Twenty-two.

118
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:28,400
He whispered a number. Twenty-two.
I didn't ask what it meant.

119
00:07:28,870 --> 00:07:31,710
All the Sun's light, sent to one star.

120
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:42,095
On Day 365, we all went up to
the surface together.

121
00:07:42,565 --> 00:07:45,330
MR. ITO: I need to point this at the Sun.

122
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:49,314
MIRA: One whole lap around the
Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

123
00:07:49,784 --> 00:07:51,999
MR. ITO: It's coming from the Sun.

124
00:07:52,469 --> 00:07:53,254
One.

125
00:07:54,004 --> 00:07:54,539
Two.

126
00:07:55,289 --> 00:07:56,024
Three.

127
00:07:57,194 --> 00:07:59,259
Mercury. Venus. Earth.

128
00:07:59,729 --> 00:08:01,469
It stopped at three.

129
00:08:02,739 --> 00:08:03,947
MIRA: That's us.

130
00:08:05,417 --> 00:08:06,382
MR. ITO: No.

131
00:08:07,332 --> 00:08:09,872
It isn't counting planets.

132
00:08:22,142 --> 00:08:27,057
The man in the observatory was Mr.
Ito. Forty-four years ago.

133
00:08:27,927 --> 00:08:31,142
He found the lines, and the
star they pointed to.

134
00:08:31,612 --> 00:08:34,202
And he knocked. One. Two. Three.

135
00:08:35,072 --> 00:08:39,187
Twenty-two years to get there.
Twenty-two years to come back.

136
00:08:39,657 --> 00:08:43,872
MR. ITO: It's been knocking back since
the morning the Sun went out.

137
00:08:44,342 --> 00:08:48,057
I was the only one who
knew where to listen.

138
00:08:48,527 --> 00:08:50,567
It isn't counting.

139
00:08:51,217 --> 00:08:52,982
It's answering.

140
00:08:54,252 --> 00:08:56,417
I'm sorry, Mira.

141
00:08:58,087 --> 00:09:00,977
That night, Mr. Ito told the world.

142
00:09:01,447 --> 00:09:03,707
Every radio on Earth heard it.

143
00:09:06,177 --> 00:09:11,012
Every morning for forty-four years,
Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

144
00:09:12,082 --> 00:09:15,117
I used to think it was
because he liked it.

145
00:09:16,987 --> 00:09:19,427
Whoever answered is still out there.

146
00:09:19,777 --> 00:09:22,762
What would you say back? Tell
us in the comments.
